def convert_durations(durations):
    total_seconds = []
    for duration in durations:
        minutes, seconds = map(int, duration.split(':'))
        total_seconds.append(minutes * 60 + seconds)
    return total_seconds

Below is a simple `Card` class (Note: This solution assumes that the ranking system used in poker is "Higher number and higher alphabet (suit) is stronger". In real poker games, the suit doesn't contribute to the ranking. Also note that for simplicity, the rank in this solution is using number 1 - 13 instead of using "Ace", "2", "3", ..., "Queen", "King".):

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class Card implements Comparable<Card> {
    private final String suit;
    private final int rank;

    public Card(String suit, int rank) {
        this.suit = suit;
        this.rank = rank;
    }

    public String getSuit() {
        return suit;
    }

    public int getRank() {
        return rank;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Card other) {
        if (this.rank > other.rank) {
            return 1;
        } else if (this.rank < other.rank) {
            return -1;
        } else { // rank is equal
            return this.suit.compareTo(other.suit);
        }
    }

    public static List<Card> shuffle(List<Card> cards) {
        Collections.shuffle(cards);
        return cards;
    }
}
```

With this class created, you can now create a `Card` object and compare them like so:

```java
